# Author-typed site copy

## Doctrine

AI may suggest wording in chat. The author types every word that lands in the repo as reader-facing copy.

Exceptions: titles, and functional UI only (nav items, button labels, form labels, placeholders, errors, loading states, short status labels).

A reader-facing string longer than two sentences or twenty words is manual. That includes meta descriptions, alt text, empty states, footer lines, card blurbs, trust copy, about text, and SEO descriptions. A short manifesto is still narrative. If it is not a title and not a functional control, the author writes it. Leave it empty.

Agents do not translate. An empty German string stays empty. Agents do not rewrite existing sentences to change voice or form of address.

Agents do not fill a scaffold because a page looks bare.

## Agents may

- Create empty content scaffolds: frontmatter, the existing or author-supplied title, `<!-- write here -->` markers
- Fix broken links, markdown syntax, and schema/frontmatter field names
- Write titles and functional UI labels within the length gate
- Maintain technical docs (`AI-USAGE.md`, README stack sections, Docker notes, this rule)

## Agents must never

- Draft, rewrite, polish, or translate reader-facing copy into the repo
- Invent locale adaptations of author prose
- Fill scaffolds, empty states, or bare pages with narrative text

## If asked to write or polish prose

Stop. Say that site copy is author-typed. Offer to scaffold an empty file or list keys in [`WRITING.md`](../../WRITING.md). Suggestions in chat are fine; do not write them into the repo.

